SDAutoLogin on Debian and Ubuntu
================================

What the package installs
-------------------------

  /usr/bin/SDAutoLogin                       the program, built without a tray
  /lib/systemd/system/sdautologin.service    the service that runs it
  /usr/lib/sysusers.d/sdautologin.conf       the user it runs as

The service starts with the machine, checks the campus session every few seconds
and signs back in whenever it was dropped. It never signs the session out, and it
has no tray icon: this package is for a machine that is reached over SSH or has
no desktop at all. For a desktop, use the archive from the project page instead.

Giving it an account
--------------------

A service has nobody to answer the setup wizard, so the account is written down
once. The program reads /var/lib/sdautologin/config.json, and the line below
writes that file:

  sudo -u sdautologin SDAutoLogin --home=/var/lib/sdautologin \
       --username=s1234567 --password=SECRET --isp=@zgyd --save config --show
  sudo systemctl restart sdautologin

Leave the service suffix out for the campus network itself. Where the settings
page is easier than the command line, add --settings=127.0.0.1:8088 to the
ExecStart line of the unit ("systemctl edit sdautologin"), reach it through an
SSH tunnel, and write the account there. The page is opened with the address the
log prints ("journalctl -u sdautologin") and not with the bare port: the address
carries a one time token, it opens the page once, and the newest line is the one
that works.

Settings and log
----------------

  /var/lib/sdautologin/config.json      the settings, the password only when it
                                        was asked to keep it
  /var/lib/sdautologin/logs/app.log     the log, rotated when it passes 4 MiB

Both stay where they are when the package is removed or purged, since the account
in them is not the package's to throw away.
